(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-micro-exam-1-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"Microbiology Exam 1 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which antibody dominates the secondary response, and which follows it?","choices":["A much higher titre of IgG","IgM first, then IgG","IgA first, then IgE","IgD alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — the reverse of the primary response's order of prominence. Followed by gradual IgM. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"What is the difference between selective and differential media?","choices":["Selective contains agents that inhibit some microbes and encourage the desired ones; differential allows several types to grow and displays visible differences between them","Selective is always synthetic; differential is always complex","Selective supports fastidious organisms; differential defines every ingredient","Selective allows several types of microbes to grow and displays visible differences between them; differential contains agents that inhibit some microbes and encourage others"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. One narrows what grows, the other makes what grows distinguishable. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Which human cells DO NOT carry MHC receptors?","choices":["B lymphocytes","Neutrophils","Red blood cells","Macrophages"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — they are found on all cells except erythrocytes. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What does interferon GAMMA do?","choices":["Inhibits cancer, stimulates B cells, activates macrophages","Activates natural killer cells","Assists B and T cell maturation","Blocks the complement cascade"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — and it increases macrophage effectiveness. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Isoniazid and ethambutol both act on mycobacterial cell walls. How do they differ?","choices":["Isoniazid binds the 30S subunit; ethambutol binds the 50S","Isoniazid inhibits DNA gyrase; ethambutol inhibits bacterial RNA polymerase","Isoniazid inhibits mycolic acid synthesis; ethambutol inhibits its incorporation","Isoniazid is topical; ethambutol is parenteral"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mycobacteria have different cell walls, which is why they need their own agents. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What is a fomite?","choices":["An inanimate object that carries infection","A live animal that transmits disease","A person carrying without symptoms","An airborne droplet nucleus"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, grouped with vehicles under indirect contact. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which organisms are typically involved in superinfection?","choices":["Escherichia coli and Salmonella enterica","Candida albicans, Clostridioides difficile, hepatitis C virus, human immunodeficiency virus, Aspergillus and resistant staphylococci","Streptococcus pyogenes, Neisseria meningitidis, Haemophilus influenzae, Streptococcus pneumoniae and Moraxella catarrhalis","Mycobacterium tuberculosis and Treponema pallidum"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The mix of fungi, bacteria and viruses is the point — reduced normal flora opens the door broadly. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Which non-living reservoir is defined as a surface or object?","choices":["Fomites","Soil","Water","Air"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Responses that stimulate T cells are called what?","choices":["Passive immunity","Cell-mediated immunity","Antibody-mediated immunity","Humoral-mediated immunity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What is the ONE requirement of natural killer cell receptors?","choices":["They must bind complement","They must be identical on every natural killer cell","They must inhibit the cell from killing healthy self-cells","They must recognize a specific antigen"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — killing is a balance of activating against inhibitory signals. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"What do COMPETENT cells do in transformation?","choices":["Inject DNA into neighboring cells","Package DNA into viral particles","Excise resistance genes and destroy them","Take up DNA fragments from their environment"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What percentage are LYMPHOCYTES, and which of them belong to INNATE immunity?","choices":["55 to 90 percent; the T cells","3 to 7 percent; all of them","20 to 35 percent","20 to 35 percent; the B cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — B and T cells are adaptive. The non-B non-T lymphocytes, including natural killer cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Who must be educated about cleaning and disinfection policy?","choices":["Only nurses","Only infection control staff","All staff, without exception","Only doctors"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Outside a clinical setting, where else is a fomite-filled room found?","choices":["Only in laboratories","A hotel room or aeroplane","Only in intensive care","Only in operating theaters"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a public restroom too. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which fragment initiates the membrane attack complex?","choices":["C3b","C5b","C5a","C3a"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"Airborne transmission is described as usually being what kind of phenomenon?","choices":["An indoor one","An outdoor one","A tropical one","A seasonal one only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — though not always. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why is treatment of eukaryotic pathogens often more difficult than treatment of bacteria?","choices":["Because eukaryotic pathogens lack a cell wall to target","Because eukaryotic pathogens replicate considerably faster than bacteria do, so they outpace the drug before an effective concentration is reached","Because cell structure and some physiology resemble those of the human host, so there is great possibility of toxic side effects","Because eukaryotic pathogens are always intracellular"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. There are also not as many drugs in the pharmacopeia for them, precisely because selective toxicity is harder to achieve. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Peptidoglycan is a polymer of which alternating units?","choices":["N-acetyl glucosamine and N-acetyl muramic acid, crosslinked by a short peptide bridge","Teichoic acid and lipoteichoic acid, crosslinked by an outer bridge of lipopolysaccharide","Crystal violet and safranin","Lipid A and core polysaccharide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The crosslink runs between the terminal D-alanine and the penultimate diamino-containing amino acid. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Which is the FIRST of the suggested best practices for disinfecting non-critical surfaces?","choices":["Monitor compliance with feedback","Implement no-touch decontamination technology","Educate all staff","Standardize cleaning policy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which cells make the SHORT pentraxins, and which two are named?","choices":["Hepatocytes","Neutrophils — defensins and lysozyme","T cells — interferon gamma and interleukin 2","Fibroblasts — interferon beta"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The long pentraxins come from myeloid, endothelial and epithelial cells. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"What size of molecule is MOST antigenic?","choices":["Molecules of any size equally","Only nucleic acids","Anything over 100,000 molecular weight","Small molecules under 1,000 molecular weight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, and most of them are large proteins. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"How does resident microflora benefit the host?","choices":["By producing all essential vitamins","By neutralizing bacterial toxins","By stimulating antibody production directly","By preventing overgrowth of harmful microbes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — competitive exclusion. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is a deoxyribonucleic acid mutation?","choices":["Transfer of a plasmid from one bacterial cell to another, which may then impact the phenotype","Uptake of naked DNA from the environment","A change in the base sequence of the genome, which may then impact the phenotype of the organism","Integration of a viral genome as a prophage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mutations occur naturally at a low level but may be induced by radiation, chemicals or viruses, and may be beneficial, neutral or deleterious. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Broad and narrow spectrum refer to what?","choices":["The number of body sites a drug can reach","The range of doses between efficacy and toxicity","The number of distinct resistance mechanisms a drug is able to overcome in practice","The range of organisms the drug will affect — broad meaning a large range, narrow a small one"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Spectrum of activity is one of the listed selection criteria. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Vancomycin is produced by which organism, and how does it act?","choices":["Saccharopolyspora erythraea; it prevents amino acid translocation","Streptomyces venezuelae; it binds the 50S subunit","Amycolatopsis orientalis; a glycopeptide that binds NAG and NAM subunits and prevents their incorporation into the wall","Paenibacillus polymyxa; a polypeptide that disrupts the outer membrane by attaching directly to its lipid components"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is an important last line against antibiotic-resistant Staphylococcus aureus. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"What is true of the lower respiratory tract?","choices":["It carries Neisseria meningitidis normally","It is colonized by anaerobes","It has the same flora as the mouth","It is essentially sterile"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and colonization there means trouble. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"How does urine flow act as a barrier, beyond flushing?","choices":["It contains mucins","It is rich in complement","Osmolality and pH","It contains keratinase"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — mechanical flush plus two chemical properties. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What is the ratio of non-human to human cells in the body?","choices":["Non-human cells slightly outnumber human ones","Human cells outnumber non-human ten to one","They are exactly equal","Non-human cells are a tiny fraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: roughly 38 to 39 trillion against about 30 trillion. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which organisms are the most common oral residents?","choices":["Coliforms","Aerobic streptococci","Strict anaerobes","Staphylococci"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which is a nonliving reservoir?","choices":["Medical personnel","Soil and water","Asymptomatic carriers","Mosquitoes and ticks"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Blood agar and mannitol salt agar are each given as examples of which media categories?","choices":["Blood agar is enriched and differential; mannitol salt agar is selective and differential","Blood agar is synthetic; mannitol salt agar is complex","Both are general purpose media","Blood agar is selective and differential; mannitol salt agar is enriched and differential"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Blood agar's differential property is the gamma, beta and alpha hemolysis patterns. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What did Alexander Fleming observe in 1928, and who turned it into a usable therapy?","choices":["Inhibition of anthrax bacilli by mold growth; Louis Pasteur developed the therapy","That mold cured guinea pigs infected with typhoid; Ernest Duchesne produced the drug","That Streptomyces produced over twenty antibiotics; Selman Waksman commercialized them, coined the term antibiotic, and won the Nobel prize in 1952","Inhibition of Staphylococcus on plates contaminated with Penicillium mold; Howard Florey and Ernst Chain produced a stable clinical form in 1939"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. All three shared the Nobel prize in medicine in 1945. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What does alpha interferon do in viral hepatitis, and what does adding polyethylene glycol change?","choices":["It blocks viral attachment; polyethylene glycol broadens the spectrum","It induces a transition from the replicating to the nonreplicating form of the virus; polyethylene glycol gives a finite treatment duration but greater adverse effects","It promotes interferon production topically; polyethylene glycol makes it oral","It inhibits neuraminidase and so stops viral escape from the host cell; adding polyethylene glycol prolongs the half-life with no accompanying disadvantage"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is the trade-off. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which environmental factors contribute to resistance?","choices":["Deforestation and loss of microbial biodiversity","Environmental warming, with potential correlations to pesticide use in food production and to microplastic contamination","Ultraviolet radiation and ozone depletion","Soil acidification, heavy metal contamination of groundwater, and progressive loss of environmental microbial diversity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The latter two are explicitly framed as potential correlations rather than established causes. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What are the three shapes of bacteria, and why does shape matter?","choices":["Round, flat and segmented — because shape determines Gram reaction","Yeast, hyphal and pleomorphic — because shape determines motility","Cocci, bacilli and spiral — because shape is used in identification","Cocci, bacilli and filamentous — because shape determines virulence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Spiral covers helical, comma, twisted rod and spirochete forms. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which organisms are most commonly involved in nosocomial infection?","choices":["Staphylococci and streptococci only","Strict anaerobes and spore formers","Respiratory viruses and yeasts by themselves","Escherichia coli, Pseudomonas and Klebsiella"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, with staphylococci, streptococci and yeasts. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Which immunity does VACCINATION confer?","choices":["Artificial active","Artificial passive","Natural active","Natural passive"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Degree and duration of protection vary with each disease. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"What is the effect of penicillin's action on penicillin-binding proteins?","choices":["It prevents dephosphorylation of the membrane transporter","It prevents peptidoglycan monomers from being transported across the cell membrane","It inactivates proteins on the cell membrane involved in cell wall synthesis and maintenance","It binds NAG and NAM subunits so they cannot be incorporated"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Inhibition of transpeptidase then blocks formation of the peptide cross links specifically. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What happens to albumin in the acute-phase response?","choices":["It is converted into C-reactive protein","It DECREASES, while defensive plasma proteins increase","It increases along with the defensive proteins","It is unchanged"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — the liver shifts its output. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Which item appears on the Kanamori list of patient-care fomites?","choices":["The staff rota","The ultrasound probe and its gel","The patient's medication chart","The ward noticeboard"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — with stethoscopes, thermometers and blood pressure monitors. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Is the induced innate response specific to the type of invader?","choices":["Yes — it is tailored to each pathogen","Only for viruses","Only after four days","No — it is still nonspecific"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, though it uses more extensive pattern recognition. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"Which portion of lipopolysaccharide carries endotoxin activity, and which is used in bacterial classification?","choices":["Lipid A carries endotoxin activity; O-polysaccharide is used in classification","Both functions belong to the core polysaccharide","Core polysaccharide carries endotoxin; Lipid A is used in classification","O-polysaccharide carries endotoxin; core polysaccharide is used in classification"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both attributions are given explicitly in the description of the outer membrane. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What is the function of a leukocidin?","choices":["Dissolving connective tissue","Clotting plasma","Blocking antibody binding","Destroying leukocytes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct; the suffix means to kill. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"What distinguishes an aseptic or sterile condition from disinfection?","choices":["Aseptic uses heat; disinfection uses chemicals","Aseptic or sterile means removal of ALL forms of microbial contamination; disinfection means elimination of SOME forms","Aseptic or sterile means elimination of SOME forms of microbial contamination; disinfection means removal of ALL forms","Aseptic applies to tissues; disinfection applies to inanimate objects"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lowering numbers rather than total elimination may be good enough depending on circumstances. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which bacteria are the source of endotoxin?","choices":["Gram-positive bacteria","Only spore formers","Only anaerobes","Gram-negative bacteria"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — lipid A is part of their outer membrane lipopolysaccharide. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which MHC class is found on macrophages, antigen-presenting cells and B lymphocytes?","choices":["Class I","Class III","Class IV","Class II"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the immune regulatory receptors. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"What is antimicrobial resistance?","choices":["The emergence of a secondary infection during therapy","The ability of a microorganism to avoid the harmful effects of an antibiotic by destroying it, transporting it out of the cell, or undergoing changes that block its effects","The failure of a drug to reach the target site at an effective concentration, whether through poor absorption, rapid clearance or barriers to distribution","The inhibition of growth without killing, as opposed to bactericidal action"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. All three routes appear again later as the cellular and biochemical mechanisms. — Lecture Number 2  - Antibiotics and Resistance - Dr. Webster.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which two immunoglobulins bind to B cells?","choices":["IgM and IgD","IgG and IgA","IgE and IgG","IgA and IgE"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — IgD's main function, and one IgM shares. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"Why does the viral share of infections matter for treatment?","choices":["Viruses cannot be transmitted by fomites","Viral disease cannot be cured with antibiotics","Viruses always respond to antibiotics","Viral infections never require treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — prevention relies on vaccines and antivirals. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the infectious dose fifty describe?","choices":["The dose at which symptoms first appear","Half the minimum number needed for infection","The dose infecting half of a given population","The dose killing half of those infected"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, the standard expression of infectious dose. — PAJ5200.Microbe-human-interactions(1).pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What distinguishes INDIRECT contact transmission?","choices":["It only involves respiratory droplets","A surface comes between them","It requires an insect vector","It only occurs in hospitals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — that is fomite transmission. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"Which immunoglobulin is the FIRST RESPONDER of the primary response, and an important complement fixer?","choices":["IgG","IgA","IgD","IgM"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and it can bind to B cells. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the SECOND line of defense based on?","choices":["Immunological memory","Vaccination","Protective cells","Barriers at the portal of entry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — and like the first line it is nonspecific. Physiological processes and antimicrobial substances. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What are the six steps of phage replication in order?","choices":["Adsorption, penetration, replication, assembly, maturation, release","Adsorption, replication, penetration, maturation, assembly, release","Uncoating, adsorption, penetration, replication, assembly, release","Penetration, adsorption, assembly, replication, maturation, release"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Adsorption is binding to a specific molecule on the host cell; release lets the viruses infect other cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which step of the Gram stain technique is the most critical?","choices":["Decolorizing with acetone alcohol for about ten seconds","Flooding the slide with crystal violet for one full minute","Flooding with Gram's iodine for one minute","Flooding with safranin for one minute"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It is called the most critical step and the one most affected by technical variations in timing and reagents. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which consequence of lysogeny has direct clinical relevance?","choices":["The bacterium loses its plasmids","The host bacterial cell is destroyed, releasing multiple copies of the virus","The bacterium may acquire new traits, such as the ability to make a toxin","The bacterium becomes unable to divide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Integration as prophage can hand the bacterium a new capability, and it also becomes immune to reinfection. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What is an inflammasome, and what does it activate?","choices":["A granule inside the neutrophil","A protein complex that activates interleukin 1 beta","A receptor that detects viral RNA","A complement convertase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and in large quantities. — Lecture 5  Dr. Webster Nonspecific Immunity.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Which cells does IgE bind, and what does that produce?","choices":["B cells — triggering their activation","Neutrophils — driving phagocytosis","Erythrocytes — causing agglutination","Mast cells and basophils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It mediates asthma and anaphylaxis, usually through histamine from the mast cells. Stimulating the inflammatory response. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What does interleukin 2 do once the T helper cell is activated?","choices":["Triggers fever","Opsonizes bacteria","Lyses virally infected cells","Activates B cells and other T cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dysregulation of it can contribute to lupus and rheumatoid arthritis. Enhances T helper function. — PAJ5200.Specific_Immunity-2.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What is a biofilm?","choices":["A single-species colony on an agar plate","A protective coating applied to hospital surfaces","A layer of dead cells with no clinical effect","A resistant polymicrobial network"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — Staphylococcus and Pseudomonas produce them. — PAJ5200.Transmission_of_microbes-2.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
